ned uh it's the baltic countries  that are not that far from here  is it's finland was the very long coast  a very long coastline and borderline  towards russia  so danes feel that this war changes  everything for them it changes their  their feeling uh that they need  togetherness with others and that is a  totally new step and a real sea change  in in danish politics so is it fair to  say then that this plays into the  overall nordic shift in defense policy  uh with its neighbors sweden and finland  deciding that they want to join nato  oh absolutely because danes now think  okay finland and sweden come into nato  that means that we can form one big  defense family here in the north take in  the baltic countries norway of course  sweden finland denmark  the countries are not that big taken by  themselves but together  they can form some political they can  have some political weight and they can  sort of really sort of reinforce each  other they can have common project  objects they can sort of stand up for  each other and that really means  something to the to the self-confidence  of of danes and they are really ready  now to sort of give up all a lot of the  resilience they had in the past  against this togetherness  and it means that they have become the  slightest slightest touch more  pro-european  dw correspondent barbara faisal in  copenhagen thanks as always for your  reporting  well we've just heard sweden and finland  are pushing ahead with their landmark  bids to join nato for sweden joining the  military alliance would mean giving up  more than two centuries of neutrality  public opinion in favor of joining grew  to around 50 percent after russia  invaded ukraine  but nato member turkey opposes the bids  turkish president tayyip erdogan accuses  sweden of supporting terrorists and is  angry about stockholm's embargo on arms  sales to turkey  speaking exclusively to dw news the  swedish defense minister peter hulkfist  explained what was behind his country's  historic shift  the situation  before 24th of february was one  situation and a situation after the  russian attack against ukraine was an is  another um  we have a situation when we have a  real war in our neighborhood and  that has changed the situation  fundamentally and  we have also decided after security  analysis to go to nato membership  together with finland our values is the  same values at other countries in europe  for example germany and  and others that we we try to create  peace and we try to create peaceful  solutions but we are not naive we see  the development around us and  we must also make conclusions from from  that situation now we have the  first big war in our neighborhood since  the second world war so there is a  fundamental change and  and it's 100 percent russia's  responsibility  the swedish defense minister minister  pedro hold speaking there
 

